`]}),e.jsxs(a,{title:"A weight vector sets how often each domain is seen",children:[e.jsx(t.p,{children:"The stages so far act on documents: filtering decides which documents exist in the corpus, and deduplication decides which of them are distinct. Mixing removes nothing. It decides how often the model sees each part of what is left, and it is the only stage that can take a domain past one epoch, because it alone sets how many tokens a run draws from each source."}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["Split the corpus into ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"m"})," domains, such as web text, code and math. Domain ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"i"})," holds ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"N_i"})," unique tokens, and a training run reads ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"R"})," tokens in total. A mixture is a weight vector ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"p = (p_1, …, p_m)"})," whose weights are non-negative and sum to one. Domain ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"i"})," then supplies ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"p_i · R"})," of the ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"R"})," training tokens. The set of all such vectors is the probability simplex ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"Δ^(m−1)"}),". Its dimension is ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"m − 1"})," because the sum fixes the last weight once the others are chosen, so raising one weight lowers the others."]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["One weight fixes two exposure numbers. Suppose ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"R"})," is 100B tokens and a math domain holds ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"N_i = 2B"})," unique tokens. Setting ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"p_i = 0.05"})," draws ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"0.05 × 100B = 5B"})," tokens from a 2B-token domain, which is 2.5 passes over it. The effective epochs of domain ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"i"})," are the tokens the run samples from it divided by its unique tokens, ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"p_i · R / N_i"}),"."]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["Two defaults suggest themselves, and neither settles the choice. The natural distribution ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"p₀"})," sets each weight in proportion to the domain's size, ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"(p₀)_i = N_i / (N_1 + … + N_m)"}),". Under it ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"p_i · R / N_i"})," is the same for every domain, so every domain gets the same number of passes and its share of the budget equals its share of the pool. The natural distribution therefore underexposes small domains: one that matters, such as math, gets a small share whatever its value. It is also a point of the simplex, a mixture like any other, so comparing a method against it compares two mixtures."]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["The other default sets the weights by hand. GPT-3 sampled the sources its authors judged higher in quality more often than their size alone would ",e.jsx(i,{id:"brown2020language",children:"Brown et al., 2020"}),". A practitioner's prior about books or math is often right. Its weakness is resolution: one hand-built vector fixes ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"m"})," numbers at once, and one downstream score cannot tell you which of the ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"m"})," judgments produced the result."]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:['Olmix, a framework for data mixing during model development, poses two problems: choosing a mixture for a fixed domain set, and updating it as the domain set changes, which the optional slide "Reuse the mixture when domains change" describes. The diagram redraws the first: a domain set enters, a mixing method runs, and one weight vector comes out ',e.jsx(i,{id:"chen2026olmix",children:"Chen et al., 2026"}),". The four questions in its lower panel are the choices a mixing method leaves open: the size and number of the proxy models, the surrogate that predicts downstream loss from a mixture, the granularity at which that surrogate is fitted, and the solver together with where the repetition cap enters, in the swarm or in the solve."]})]})]}),e.jsxs(n,{title:"The objective averages bits per byte over tasks",accent:4,number:"23",children:[e.jsx(l,{math:"p^\\star = \\arg\\min_{p \\in \\Delta^{m-1}} \\frac{1}{n}\\sum_{i=1}^{n} f_i(\\mathrm{LM}(S, R, p))",label:"(4)"}),e.jsxs(t.ul,{children:[`
