`]}),e.jsxs(a,{title:"A proxy's winning mixture is a hypothesis about the target scale",children:[e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["MAI-Thinking-1's report compares candidate mixtures along a ladder of runs at increasing scale. At 5B active parameters (the parameters a mixture-of-experts model uses per token), the STEM-heavy mixture leads. During a larger run the curves cross, and from then on the code-heavy mixture leads ",e.jsx(i,{id:"microsoftai2026maithinking1",children:"Microsoft AI Team, 2026"}),". A small proxy can therefore rank mixtures wrongly even when every measurement in it is sound, and the surrogate's winner, picked at proxy scale, is a hypothesis about the target scale until a larger run confirms it."]}),e.jsx(t.p,{children:"The crossing shows that an ordering measured at one rung of a ladder can invert at a larger one. The report places the crossing during the larger run and gives no parameter count for it, so we can quote no crossover scale, and no scale beyond which an ordering holds. STEM-heavy and code-heavy are this ladder's two candidates under one domain set, so the result ranks neither family in general."}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["The mitigation the report gives is hierarchical search plus validation at larger scales of the ladder ",e.jsx(i,{id:"microsoftai2026maithinking1",children:"Microsoft AI Team, 2026"}),"."]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["DataComp-VLM, covered in the optional Bonus 5, reports a ranking that flips with scale for vision-language data mixtures ",e.jsx(i,{id:"farina2026dcvlm",children:"Farina et al., 2026"}),"."]})]})]})]}),`
