`]}),e.jsxs(a,{title:"Privacy filtering and decontamination protect different things, and both need tests",children:[e.jsx(t.p,{children:"PII filtering and decontamination both remove text from a corpus, but they protect different things. PII filtering protects people by keeping personal data, such as email addresses, out of the corpus. Decontamination protects a measurement by keeping benchmark items out, so that a score stays meaningful."}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["FineWeb anonymizes email addresses and public IP addresses with regular expressions ",e.jsx(i,{id:"penedo2024fineweb",children:"Penedo et al., 2024"}),". The filter of olmOCR, OLMo 3's pipeline for academic PDFs, is model-based and aware of document types: it asks whether a document is of a type intended for public dissemination ",e.jsx(i,{id:"ettinger2025olmo3",children:"Ettinger et al., 2025"}),'. A pattern edits strings inside a page, and the olmOCR filter decides about a whole document. The secret and PII redaction of "Code needs a front end of its own" is the same lane for code. Polite crawling, robots.txt, opt-outs and copyright handling are governance. They decide what may be collected and kept, and none of them is evidence that benchmark items were removed.']}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["OLMo 3's report records a SQuAD and DROP preprocessing failure in its ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"decon"})," workflow ",e.jsx(i,{id:"ettinger2025olmo3",children:"Ettinger et al., 2025"}),". Together with GPT-3's partial-removal bug, this failure shows why you test a decontamination pipeline against known positives, training text you know overlaps a benchmark, and why you inspect the cleaned evaluation subsets it produces. The legal side of data collection is outside this lecture, and CS336's lecture 14 treats it ",e.jsx(i,{id:"cs336-2026",children:"Hashimoto & Liang, 2026"}),"."]})]})]})]}),`
